---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import DateCalculator from '@components/islands/DateCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';

const calc = getCalculator('everyday', 'date-calculator')!;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How do I calculate the number of days between two dates?',
    answer:
      'Enter a start date and an end date in the "Days between two dates" tool. It counts every calendar day between them, including leap days, and also shows the span in years, months, days and weeks.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does the day count include both the start and end date?',
    answer:
      'By default it is the difference — end date minus start date — so January 1 to January 2 is 1 day. Tick "Include the end day" when you need both endpoints counted, such as total days away on a trip, and the count goes up by one.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I add or subtract time from a date?',
    answer:
      'Use the "Add or subtract from a date" task: pick a start date, choose add or subtract, and fill in any combination of years, months, weeks and days. Boxes you leave blank count as zero. The resulting calendar date is shown instantly.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I count business days between two dates?',
    answer:
      'Tick "Count business days only" and the result counts Monday to Friday, skipping every weekend. Tick "Also skip US federal holidays" as well and the eleven observed federal holidays come out too — including the day a holiday is moved to when it falls on a weekend.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens when I add a month to the 31st?',
    answer:
      'The date is clamped to the last day of the target month, so one month after January 31 is February 28 — or February 29 in a leap year — rather than rolling into March. Years and months are applied first, then weeks and days, which is why a month and a day after January 31 is March 1.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I count down the days until a future date?',
    answer:
      'Enter today as the start date and the event as the end date; the "days between" result is your countdown. It updates as soon as you change either date.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Are leap years and month lengths handled correctly?',
    answer:
      'Yes. All date math is done on the calendar, so February 29th in leap years and the different lengths of each month are accounted for automatically.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="Find the number of days between two dates, or add and subtract years, months, weeks and days from any date. Count business days only if you need to, and leap years and month lengths are handled automatically."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-09' }}
>
  <DateCalculator />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>Two date tools in one</h2>
    <p>
      Pick a mode with the selector at the top. <strong>Days between two dates</strong>
      measures the duration between a start and an end date — useful for counting down to an
      event, tracking a project timeline or checking someone's age on a specific day.
      <strong>Add or subtract from a date</strong> shifts a single date forward or back by any
      combination of years, months, weeks and days, handy for deadlines, due dates and return-by
      dates. Either task can count business days instead of calendar days.
    </p>

    <h2>Finding the days between two dates</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose the <strong>days between</strong> mode.</li>
      <li>Enter a <strong>start date</strong> and an <strong>end date</strong>.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong> to see the total days, the span in weeks and days, and a breakdown in years, months and days.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The two dates are order-independent: put the later one first and the tool still reports
      the same span, simply noting the direction rather than flagging an error. Two identical
      dates return zero.
    </p>

    <h2>Counting days: the endpoint question</h2>
    <p>
      "How many days between" almost always means the difference — the later date minus the
      earlier one. From July 1 to July 10 that is <strong>9 days</strong>. If you actually want
      to include both the first and last day, as when counting the days of a trip, tick
      <strong>Include the end day</strong> and the count becomes <strong>10</strong>. Deciding
      which you need up front avoids the classic off-by-one error.
    </p>

    <h2>Counting business days</h2>
    <p>
      Tick <strong>Count business days only</strong> and the count drops the weekends, which is
      what you want for delivery windows, notice periods, court deadlines and anything else
      measured in working days. Tick <strong>Also skip US federal holidays</strong> and the
      eleven federal holidays come out too.
    </p>
    <p>
      Holidays are counted on the day they are <strong>observed</strong>, not the date in the
      statute. A fixed-date holiday that lands on a Saturday is observed the Friday before, and
      one on a Sunday the Monday after — so Independence Day 2027 falls on a Sunday and the day
      off is Monday July 5. Juneteenth is only counted from 2021, the year it became a federal
      holiday, so historical spans are not quietly shortened.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the add-or-subtract task, business days are a count of working days, so the amount goes
      in the Days box on its own. The start day itself is never counted: three business days
      from a Friday is the following Wednesday, and asking from the Saturday lands on the same
      Wednesday.
    </p>

    <h2>Adding or subtracting from a date</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Switch to the <strong>add or subtract</strong> mode.</li>
      <li>Enter a <strong>start date</strong> and choose <strong>add</strong> or <strong>subtract</strong>.</li>
      <li>Fill in any combination of <strong>years, months, weeks and days</strong>. A box left blank counts as zero; at least one must be filled in.</li>
      <li>The resulting calendar date appears, with the weekday spelled out.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Because the count is applied straight to the calendar, month lengths and leap days are
      handled for you — there is no need to work out which months have 30 or 31 days.
    </p>

    <h2>What "one month later" means</h2>
    <p>
      Months are not all the same length, so adding one to the 31st has to land somewhere. The
      answer here is the <strong>last day of the target month</strong>: one month after January
      31 is February 28, or February 29 in a leap year, rather than rolling over into March.
      That is what a calendar does when you page forward a month, and it keeps the step
      reversible.
    </p>
    <p>
      Years and months are applied first, then weeks and days. That order matters at month ends:
      <strong>one month and one day</strong> after January 31 is March 1 — the month step clamps
      to February 28 first, and the day is added to that.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      From <strong>15 January 2026</strong> to <strong>10 August 2026</strong> is
      <strong>207 days</strong> — that is <strong>29 weeks and 4 days</strong>, or 6 months and
      26 days in the calendar breakdown, and <strong>147 business days</strong> once weekends
      come out. Switch to the other mode and adding <strong>90 days</strong> to 10 August 2026
      lands on <strong>8 November 2026</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>Accurate calendar math</h2>
    <p>
      Because months vary in length and leap years add a day, counting dates by hand is
      error-prone. This calculator works directly on the calendar, so every result is exact no
      matter which dates you choose, and the total-day count and the year-month-day breakdown
      always agree. Looking for an exact age from a birth date instead? Try the
      <a href="/everyday/age-calculator">age calculator</a>; to add up spans of hours and
      minutes, use the <a href="/everyday/time-calculator">time calculator</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
